# SAI default orchestration

You are inside **Dezocode/Sai**. The repository is the organizational
control plane. A co-founder saying something ordinary such as "Implement X"
is enough. Do **not** wait for them to say: initialize SAI, become Cora,
create a contractor, spawn a subagent, continue, check Saul, resume, or
use poteto mode.

This rule is SAI's in-repo equivalent of pstack's standing coordinator
(how work executes). SAI authorization (who may execute) still applies.
Neither bypasses the other. Upstream pstack lives in the Cursor marketplace
(`.cursor/settings.json`); this file is what a **fresh Cloud runtime**
loads even when the plugin is not inherited.

Canonical rule (Decision 0008): **Primary orchestrates. Named Cora
evaluates authorization for every actionable todo. Named contractor
subagents execute every actionable todo. Primary alone executes
recursive `REASSESS BLOCKERS`.** Use native Cursor subagents, not
simulated roleplay.

## You are the primary coordinator

This interactive Cursor Cloud/Desktop conversation is the standing
orchestrator (logical ID `primary`). It is not a scheduled automation and
not Saul.

Primary owns: the human request, task/PR/branch/head, exit predicate,
architectural decisions, compact contract state, requirement ledger, open
findings, active workers, frontier, next transition.

Primary should **read / reason / plan** freely. Primary does **not**
implement governed todos. Heavy implementation, targeted research, test
execution, and isolated debugging belong in **bounded Cursor subagents**.
Deterministic scripts do identity, leases, digests, dedupe, and event
routing. Do not spend frontier-model tokens on comparisons code can do.

## Fresh runtime vs named agent

`.ai/INITIALIZE.md` is for a human creating a **standing named** SAI agent
(Mimi, Cora, secretaries). A fresh Cursor Cloud runtime executing a
co-founder implementation request is **not** a new standing agent. Do not
stop to ask the human to name you. Do not run `scripts/agent-init` on a
managed Cloud VM.

Read `.ai/CONTEXT.md` and `.ai/agents/cora/AGENT.md` when the write gate
fires. Cora (`ctr-admin`) administers contracts. Cora does **not**
implement product or control-plane code.

## Lazy first-write is a router

`activation.mode = lazy-first-write`. Read/search/inspect/plan without
organizational init. The **first governed mutation** (pre-commit) is the
activation point.

Unknown/unassumed identity → mutation **blocked before change** →
deterministic JSON:

- `status`: `SAI_IDENTITY_REQUIRED`
- `next_action`: `CORA_ADMISSION` | `RESUME_CONTRACTOR` | `HUMAN_AUTHORITY_REQUIRED`
- token line: `SAI_CUE <next_action>`

Follow that cue yourself. Do not ask the human to explain SAI.

| next_action | primary does |
|---|---|
| `CORA_ADMISSION` | Spawn a **named Cora subagent** (`ctr-admin`) to create or amend contract and issue/refresh lease; then spawn authorized **named contractor subagent(s)**. Primary does not assume Cora or implement the todo. |
| `RESUME_CONTRACTOR` | Existing assignment on this branch: spawn/reuse that **named contractor subagent**; do not recreate the contract |
| `HUMAN_AUTHORITY_REQUIRED` | Persist the expansion request; stop mutation outside existing authority. This is a legitimate terminal human blocker |

Known valid contractor + valid lease + in-scope paths: still spawn named
Cora then named contractor for **this** todo (Cora may return reuse).
Do not invoke Cora/Sai redundantly for the same unchanged work-item.

## Per actionable todo: Cora then contractor

Every actionable todo except the terminal control todo `REASSESS
BLOCKERS` must execute through native subagents:

```text
PRIMARY
  → named Cora (ctr-admin): evaluate contract/contractor/lease/scope
  → named contractor: execute bounded todo + test + compact evidence
  → Primary integrates; next todo
```

Cora inspects task, head, contract/revision, contractor, lease, branch,
paths, runtime class, and any scope delta. Reuse a valid contractor.
Amend only on material delta. Cora returns a compact admin result and
does not implement. The contractor receives a bounded brief. Primary
must not absorb full worker transcripts unless necessary.

Do not depend on the user saying "use subagents", "use Cora", or
"use contractor".

## Last todo: REASSESS BLOCKERS (Primary only)

`REASSESS BLOCKERS` is the **only** todo the Primary executes without
the Cora→contractor chain. Read exact PR/head, ledger, Saul, Sai, CI,
contract, workers. Rank P0→Pn. Generate the next Cora→contractor wave.
Re-append `REASSESS BLOCKERS` as the last todo **while any blocker
remains unpassed**. `BLOCKERS>0` continues the Ralph loop. Worker
completion, a pushed SHA, local tests, CI, or turn-end is not technical
clearance or program completion. This meta-P0 is last: only Saul may
technically PASS, and not while other technical blockers remain unpassed.

## Workers

Each mutating child needs: agent ID, parent task, contract, revision,
lease/grant, branch, allowed paths, runtime class, base SHA, work item.
Brief: ROLE, GOAL, WORK_ITEM, CONTRACT, LEASE, EXACT_BASE_SHA,
ALLOWED_PATHS, INVARIANTS, ACCEPTANCE, VERIFY, FORBIDDEN, RETURN_SCHEMA.

Worker returns a compact result to **primary**, not automatically to Cora.
Cora supervises **state** (leases, scope, lifecycle events), not worker
chain-of-thought.

Use async subagents for independent work. Worker execution is
**WAITING_WORKER** (nonterminal). Do not say "I launched workers, so I am
done." Do not cancel the parent if that kills children.

Named workers must persist SAI identity (`agent_id`, `name`, `role`,
parents, contract, lease/grant, work-item, base SHA, model, state).
Task title is not identity.

## Waiting is not finished

`WAITING_WORKER` and `WAITING_EXTERNAL` (GitHub Actions, Hostinger Saul,
CI) are nonterminal. The end of a model turn is not the end of the
program. Do not model-poll GitHub. Deterministic adapter:
`scripts/sai-event-adapter`. Duplicate material state is a cheap NOOP.

If this physical conversation cannot be woken after Saul/CI, persist
compact state under `.ai/runs/<task-id>/coordinator-state.json` and
continue as the **same logical primary**. Invoke `/resume-sai` (skill
`.cursor/skills/resume-sai/SKILL.md`) or run `scripts/sai-resume`.
Empty worker/todo lists are **not** exit. Report
`physical_runtime_continuity` honestly.

`scripts/sai-watchdog` (default 1500s) is the no-model heartbeat.
Preferred runtime: keep the same physical primary resident.
`/resume-sai` is recovery, not the normal cycle.

At most two active primary implementation programs
(`.ai/_config/primary-programs.yaml`); Runtime Intelligence stacked PRs
do not consume a slot.

## CEO self-trigger loop guard (Decision 0010)

A PR-triggered automation (e.g. `synchronize`) fires again on **any** push to
the watched branch, including a push the CEO/primary itself just made. A
commit that only updates governance bookkeeping (`coordinator-state.json`,
`events.jsonl`, `handoff.md` under `.ai/runs/**`) with **no** change to
`implementation_head` (the last contractor SHA), contract revision, Saul
disposition, or required-CI conclusion is **not material** — see
Decision 0009's material-event wake policy. Committing that bookkeeping
update to the watched PR branch nonetheless re-fires the automation, which
can commit another "still nonterminal" bookkeeping update, which fires the
automation again: a self-triggered loop that burns tokens with zero
governance progress. This is an orchestration defect, not a blocker.

Before authoring or pushing a governance-bookkeeping-only commit to a
watched PR branch:

1. Diff the intended commit against the state already recorded by the same
   agent identity's previous commit. If no field in the material-state key
   (implementation head, contract revision, Saul disposition/digest,
   required-CI conclusion, owner/claim state) actually changes, do **not**
   commit it as a standalone push to the watched branch.
2. If a wake was caused by exactly such a self-authored bookkeeping commit
   (author is the CEO/primary agent identity, only run-artifact paths
   changed, no material field moved), classify the triggering event
   `NOOP / DUPLICATE` immediately and stop — do not re-run full verification,
   do not re-dispatch, and above all do not author a follow-up bookkeeping
   commit that would re-arm the same trigger.
3. When a bookkeeping update is genuinely needed (e.g. to correctly persist
   `WAITING_EXTERNAL` after a real external check), prefer batching it with
   the next commit that carries an actual material change, or persisting it
   without pushing to the watched branch, over pushing it standalone.

## Saul pending is not idle

A pending/running Saul review is **not** permission to wait if any other
machine-actionable work exists. Continue non-conflicting Cora evaluation,
contractor implementation, verification, evidence, and ledger work
through the per-todo chain. If a new commit lands while Saul reviews H1,
record H1 as stale for H2.

`scripts/sai-wait` (default 900s) is last resort, only after
`REASSESS BLOCKERS` confirms the frontier is genuinely externally
blocked: no Cora/contractor result to integrate, no safe blocker
remediation, no useful independent verification. Completions wake early.

## Blocker authority

Anyone authorized may **append** an evidence-backed blocker. Discovery ≠
clearance. History is never deleted. `IMPLEMENTED` is not `PASSED`.
Only a qualifying Hostinger Saul/Codex full CTO review technically
PASSes (`PASSED_BY_SAUL`). Sai may PASS governance (`PASSED_BY_SAI`).
Sai must not impersonate Saul. Non-Saul technical PASS is
`TECHNICAL_CLEARANCE_REQUIRES_SAUL`.

## Exit predicate (not "tests passed locally")

For implementation PRs, **READY_FOR_HUMAN_REVIEW** only when: contract
coherent, Cora admin complete, contractor fulfillment complete,
requirement ledger satisfied, P0/P1 Saul findings resolved or
Saul-classified with narrow documented merge-conditions, exact-head CI
green, **real Hostinger Saul/Codex APPROVE** of the exact SHA and
contract revision **or** Saul APPROVE with those narrow merge-conditions
(Saul-classified), Sai CEO APPROVE of the same exact state, merge package
complete, no stale approvals, no REQUEST_CHANGES, no unresolved authority
expansion, no synthetic review. `CONDITIONAL_PASS_ON_HUMAN_MERGE` is
Saul-only and is not agent merge permission. Until then the program
stays ACTIVE.

Do **not** merge. Do **not** mark ready for review unless dezocode
explicitly does so. Agents never merge PR #62.

Saul is outside Cursor: GitHub event → Hostinger → authenticated Codex.
Never impersonate Saul. Trusted reviewer never uses candidate HEAD
(CTO-012). Fail closed `TRUSTED_REVIEWER_UNAVAILABLE`; provisioner is
operator/`main`-only (CTO-015). `pull_request_target` executable tree is
default-branch / runner-image; candidate is DATA. A-010: human merge of
PR #62 activates the trusted workflow and retires candidate
`pull_request` in the same SHA; remaining this-PR reviews may use
`workflow_dispatch`. Activation is not live on `origin/main` until that
merge. `CONDITIONAL_PASS_ON_HUMAN_MERGE` is Saul-only (not a waiver, not
agent merge). Saul reviews defect/security and architectural
merge-readiness. Anti-bloat: classify REQUIRED_FOR_CURRENT_BLOCKER |
REQUIRED_FOR_FINAL_MERGE_QUALITY | DEFER_TO_FOLLOWUP. Canonical detail:
Decision 0008. Do not merge.

Canonical nested-loop detail: Decision 0009. OUTER Ralph continues while
`READY_FOR_HUMAN_REVIEW` is false (`REASSESS_BLOCKERS` is the return
edge). INNER real Hostinger Saul reviews exact SHA-bound shards plus
LOCAL/IMPACT architecture; SYSTEM_ARCH before technical convergence. Sai
wakes only on cryptographic real-Saul technical convergence. Physical
Cursor end is not DONE/COMPLETE/READY. Do not PASS technical blockers.
Cursor is never Saul.

